<!-- /site-examples:listen:zig -->
```

Run a Listen tab and its matching Dial tab together to see the call
succeed. To try CapTP from a shell instead, see the
[CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')).

# [From C, or any FFI host]($section.id('from-c-or-any-ffi-host'))

The C ABI is built around `GlowcapHost`, which owns a program's CapTP sessions
and routes between them. Your program owns the event loop and
the network connection. It feeds received bytes into a Host session, takes the
actions the Host produces, and writes out the resulting frames. Every
capability is a `GlowcapReference`, an opaque handle that belongs to the Host
rather than to any one session. The ABI uses fixed-width return codes,
`ptr + len` views, and explicit release.
[Consuming glowcap from C](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md)
covers building, ownership, and a first program.

# In Rust, TypeScript, Python, or Racket

Each binding wraps the C ABI in its language's own idiom. The
[bindings overview]($link.page('bindings')) compares them, and the list at the
top links each one.

For the browser, the TypeScript binding also compiles to WebAssembly as
`@glowcap/web`. A page cannot open a listening socket, but it can accept peers
over WebRTC or through a relay. See
[In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser')).

If capabilities, locators, pipelining, or three-party handoff are new to you,
read [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) next.
